   "author_display_name": "cadaniluk",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": true,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/33281742",
   "score": 7,
   "created_utc": "2015-10-22T13:04:00Z",
   "last_edit_utc": "2015-10-22T13:19:07Z",
   "last_activity_utc": "2015-10-22T13:19:07Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "<p>There are multiple string \"types\" <code>fileName</code> may point to:</p>\n\n<ol>\n<li><p>Space returned by <code>malloc</code>, <code>calloc</code>, or <code>realloc</code>. In this case, use <code>free</code>.</p></li>\n<li><p>A string literal. If you assign <code>info.fileName = \"some string\"</code>, there is no way. The string literal is written in the executable itsself and is usually stored together with the program's code. There is a reason a string literal should be accessed by <code>const char*</code> only and C++ only allows <code>const char*</code>s to point to them.</p></li>\n<li><p>A string on the stack like <code>char str[] = \"some string\";</code>. Use curly braces to confine its scope and lifetime like that:</p>\n\n<pre><code>struct information info;\n{\n    char str[] = \"some string\";\n    info.fileName = str;\n}\nprintf(\"%s\\n\", info.fileName);\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>The <code>printf</code> call results in undefined behavior since <code>str</code> has already gone out of scope, so the string has already been deallocated.</p></li>\n</ol>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "comment",
   "post_id": 54363757,
   "parent_id": 33281700,
   "parent_type": "question",
   "author_user_id": 440558,
   "author_display_name": "Some programmer dude",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": null,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/33281700/#comment54363757_33281700",
   "score": 3,
   "created_utc": "2015-10-22T13:04:41Z",
   "edited": false,
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "You should <code>free</code> what you <code>malloc</code> (or <code>calloc</code>/<code>realloc</code> etc.), but other than that there&#39;s nothing to do."
  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 33281759,
   "parent_id": 33281700,
   "author_user_id": 648658,
   "author_display_name": "Jens",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": false,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/33281759",
   "score": 1,
   "created_utc": "2015-10-22T13:05:21Z",
   "last_edit_utc": null,
   "last_activity_utc": "2015-10-22T13:05:21Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "<p>You could use <code>foo.fileName = malloc(howmanychars);</code> and <code>free(foo.fileName);</code>.</p>\n\n<p>You cannot free the memory if you initialize <code>fileName</code> from a string literal or other non-dynamically allocated way.</p>\n\n<p>But then, freeing a handful of bytes is next to pointless, unless you need a large number of such structs/fileNames. The OS will likely <strong>not</strong> return the freed memory to other processes; the returned memory <em>may</em> be available for future memory allocations of your process.</p>\n"
  },
  {
